<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anekantavada</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as many-sidedness</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anekantavada, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for &quot;many-sidedness&quot;, is the Jain doctrine that ultimate truth and reality are complex and have multiple aspects and viewpoints. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> holds that no single statement can describe existence, and that complete knowledge (Kevala Jnana) is comprehended only by the Arihants. Jain tradition traces it to the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a> and his predecessors, while its details emerged in the 1st millennium CE from debates among Jain, Buddhist and Vedic scholars. Syadvada and nayavada later gave it logical structure. Jain texts illustrate it with the parable of the blind men and an elephant.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Anekantavada is a compound of an (not), eka (one) and anta (end or side), so that it means &quot;not one-sided&quot;. It holds that reality can be experienced but cannot be fully expressed in language. Human statements are naya, partial expressions of the truth, and any statement is valid &quot;in some respect&quot; (syat) while remaining one perspective. All knowledge claims must therefore be qualified, affirmed and denied in many ways. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, language proceeds from truth, not the reverse.</p><p>The nayavada lists seven standpoints from which judgments about reality can be made, which Umaswati names: the common-sense or universal view, the generic view, the pragmatic view, the linear view that considers a thing in the present, the verbal view, the etymological view and the view of concrete particulars. The theory of nayas emerged after about the 5th century CE and was developed extensively.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word anekantavada does not appear in the early texts the Svetambara tradition treats as canonical, though traces of the idea are found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>'s remarks there. The term is said to have been coined by Acharya Siddhasen Divakar, and the earliest comprehensive account is in the Tattvarthasutra of Acharya Umaswami, held authoritative by all Jain sects. Jain thinkers shaped the doctrine in debate with Buddhist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> schools, borrowing terms but also innovating.</p><p>Jain texts illustrate it with the parable of the blind men and the elephant. One man touches the trunk and says the animal is like a thick snake, another touches the ear and takes it for a fan, another the leg and takes it for a pillar. Later writers extended the doctrine: Acharya Haribhadra in the 8th century wrote a survey of intellectual views, and Acharya Amrtacandra in the 10th century opened a work by bowing to the principle of anekanta. In the 17th century Yaśovijaya Gaṇi advocated standing &quot;in the middle&quot;, which let him praise qualities in non-Jains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Scholars disagree about what the doctrine means for other faiths. Some Indian writers call it inclusivist, accepting non-Jain teachings as partial truths. Others say this is a later reconstruction, since classical Jain scholars saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> as the one true path and considered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> inadequate. John Koller describes it as epistemological respect for the views of others, but not relativism: it does not concede that all arguments are equal. It has also been read as non-absolutism and as &quot;intellectual ahimsa&quot;.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anekantavada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jain philosophy is known for the doctrine of <b>anekantavada</b>, “non-one-sidedness”: reality is complex, and every statement about it is true only from a particular point of view, a principle that encourages tolerance of other opinions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
